          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "From what age does guidance describe screening for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "Diabetes association guidance recommends screening all adults from **age 35**, regardless of weight. It also screens younger adults who are overweight or obese and have at least one extra risk factor. Those factors include a first-degree relative with diabetes, a higher-risk ethnic background, high blood pressure, or a history of gestational diabetes. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds*, national diabetes association guidance.)\n\n**Where authorities differ:** Preventive-services guidance, in the same document, restricts routine screening to adults **aged 35 to 70 who are overweight or obese**. The two positions lead to very different numbers of people being tested. Guidance from the first body covers everyone from 35, while the second limits screening to a narrower group. The retrieved text says this disagreement is genuine, and I can't say which position is correct.\n\n**Other details from the same guidance:** A lower body-mass-index threshold applies to people of Asian ancestry. When a screening test is normal, the usual repeat interval is three years, sooner if risk factors build up or weight increases.\n\n**Evidence strength:** The retrieved passages do not grade how strong the evidence is for either age cut-off. The preventive-services position says it is based on screening trials showing benefit in the overweight or obese group, but I can't go further than that from these passages.\n\nThis is general information, not a judgement about any individual. If you are wondering whether you should be tested, a clinician can apply these criteria to your own age, weight and history.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
